To solve this, we first recognize the series as a derivative of a geometric series.
\begin{align*} \sum_{k = 1}^\infty k x^{k - 1} &= \frac{d}{dx} \sum_{k = 1}^\infty x^k = \frac{d}{dx} \left(\frac{x}{1 - x}\right) = \frac{1}{(1 - x)^2} \end{align*}
From the original geometric series, $\sum_{k = 1}^\infty x^k = \frac{x}{1 - x},$ we get $x = \frac{1}{2}$ to get $\sum_{k = 1}^\infty \frac{1}{2^k} = 1.$
\begin{align*} \sum_{k = 1}^\infty k^2 \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^k &= \sum_{k = 1}^\infty k \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{k - 1} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right) \\ &= \frac{1}{2} \sum_{k = 1}^\infty k \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^{k - 1} \\ &= \frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{4}{(1 - \frac{1}{2})^2} \\ &= \boxed{4} \end{align*}
